 ### Embedded Error Norm Calculation
-This method computes a norm for an error vector `err` and solution vector `y` given relative tolerance `rtol` and absolute tolerance `atol` [S2, unofficial]. For a single component, it collapses to $|\text{err}| / \text{scale}$. For multi-component vectors, it computes an RMS-like norm: $\sqrt{\frac{1}{N} \sum \left(\frac{\text{err}_i}{\text{rtol} \cdot |y_i| + \text{atol}}\right)^2}$ [S2, unofficial]. Typical parameters are `rtol = 1e-6` and `atol = 0` [S2, unofficial]. Example: `err=[1e-6, 2e-6]`, `y=[1, 2]`, `rtol=1e-6`, `atol=0` yields a norm of $\approx 1.581139$ [S2, unofficial].
